  "worked_solution": "To get rid of fractions, we multiply the first equation by $3$ and the second equation by $7,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}y=2 & |\\times 3 \\\\ x+\\frac{4}{7}y=-\\frac{3}{7} & |\\times 7\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}x+7y=6 \\\\ 7x+4y=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Notice that the first equation can easily be solved for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7y & =6 \\\\ x & =6-7y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=6-7y$ into the second equation and then solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7x+4y & =-3 \\\\ 7(6-7y)+4y & =-3 \\\\ 42-49y+4y & =-3 \\\\ -45y & =-45 \\\\ y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $x,$ we substitute $y=1$ into the equation $x=6-7y$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6-7y\\text{\u00a0} \\\\  & =6-7(1) \\\\  & =6-7 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=-1$ and $y=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
